#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:1
msgid "Re-exposing Traits"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:3
msgid ""
"Sometimes we use an enum to abstract over multiple types that implement the "
"same trait, and want the enum to also re-expose the trait's interface."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:21
msgid "\"Woof! My name is {}~!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:27
msgid "\"Meow! I am {} years old\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:52
msgid "\"Fido\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:63
msgid ""
"One drawback of using an enum for dynamic polymorphism is that if our "
"underlying types (`Cat` and `Dog` in this case) implement a trait (`Pet`), "
"our wrapper enum doesn't automatically expose that same trait interface."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:67
msgid ""
"We can generally implement the trait for the wrapper enum by matching on the "
"enum and dispatching to the corresponding trait method on the underlying "
"types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:71
msgid ""
"This is dynamic dispatch, but using the enum's discriminant instead of a "
"vtable in order to lookup the correct function to call."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:74
msgid ""
"This is an ergonomic drawback of an enum vs `dyn`: The implementation of "
"`Pet` for `AnyPet` is pure boilerplate that we need to repeat each time we "
"have a situation like this, whereas `dyn` gives us this behavior purely from "
"the `Pet` impls on `Cat` and `Dog`."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:79
msgid ""
"To avoid writing this boilerplate ourselves, we can use a crate like "
"[enum_dispatch](https://crates.io/crates/enum_dispatch), which handles the "
"work of generating the wrapper enum and implementing the trait."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:83
msgid ""
"The advantage of this approach is that we retain the useful properties of an "
"enum (e.g. the ability to pattern match on it) while also exposing a way to "
"do dynamic dispatch through the trait's interface."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/traits.md:87
msgid ""
"This also enables us to use `AnyPet` with generic functions like "
"`do_pet_stuff`, which we can also do with `dyn`."
msgstr ""
